<body>
<header class="top">
  <a class="brand" href="{{.urls.home}}">GhostPaw<span class="crumb">/codex</span></a>
  <nav aria-label="codex elsewhere">
    <a href="{{.urls.demo}}">Live demo</a>
    <a href="{{.urls.github}}">GitHub</a>
    <a href="{{.urls.npm}}">npm</a>
  </nav>
</header>
<main>
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="kicker"><code>@ghostpaw/codex</code> <span class="ver">0.6.0</span></p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">When one agent saves a wrong fact, every agent trusts it.</h1>
      <p class="lede">codex keeps what your agent believes as beliefs with a source, a certainty and a history, so you can read and delete them one at a time instead of rereading MEMORY.md.</p>
      <div class="install">
        <p class="cmd-label" id="bin-label">Release binary, Linux and macOS (<a href="#binaries">every platform</a>)</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="cmd-bin" readonly value="curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GhostPawJS/codex/main/install.sh | sh" aria-labelledby="bin-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="cmd-bin">Copy</button></div>
        <p class="cmd-label" id="mcp-label">As a local MCP server</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="cmd-mcp" readonly value="ghostpaw-codex mcp" aria-labelledby="mcp-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="cmd-mcp">Copy</button></div>
      </div>
      <p class="actions"><a class="button" href="#drill">Follow one wrong fact</a> <a href="{{.urls.github}}">Read the source on GitHub</a></p>
      <p class="terms">Free, MIT licence, zero runtime dependencies. One executable and one SQLite file on your machine; npm and library use need Node.js 24 or newer.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="hero-art frame">
      <div class="scope"><picture><source media="(min-width: 52rem)" srcset="assets/images/hero-720.webp" width="720" height="540"><img src="assets/images/hero-480.webp" width="480" height="360" alt="A glowing cyan paw print on a translucent belief card with a small rose flag on its corner, older violet cards fading behind it." fetchpriority="high"></picture><span class="scan" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
      <p class="readout" aria-label="Zero runtime dependencies"><span class="big">0</span><span>runtime dependencies</span></p>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="drill" class="drill" aria-labelledby="drill-title">
    <h2 id="drill-title">One wrong fact, five moves</h2>
    <p class="intro">The hard half is forgetting.</p>
    <p class="intro">Here is one belief from the README example, from the guess that starts it to the call that deletes it. The cards show what codex 0.3.2 actually returned when we ran these calls on 2026-10-24 with a fixed clock, so the numbers are not made up for the page.</p>
    <div class="tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Steps">
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-1" aria-controls="step-1" aria-selected="true"><span>1</span> remember</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-2" aria-controls="step-2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"><span>2</span> recall</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-3" aria-controls="step-3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"><span>3</span> correct</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-4" aria-controls="step-4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"><span>4</span> day 90</button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-5" aria-controls="step-5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"><span>5</span> retire</button>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="step-1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-1">
      <div class="step-text">
        <h3>An agent saves a guess</h3>
        <p>One session guesses that the API still has a REST fallback and saves it. The belief is marked inferred, starts at 50% certainty and is flagged low trust before anyone has read it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="belief flagged">
        <p class="belief-id">Belief #1 · fact</p>
        <p class="claim">The API uses GraphQL with a REST fallback.</p>
        <dl class="fields">
          <div><dt>source</dt><dd>inferred</dd></div>
          <div><dt>certainty</dt><dd>50%</dd></div>
          <div><dt>evidence</dt><dd>1</dd></div>
          <div><dt>freshness</dt><dd>100%</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="flags"><span class="flag">low trust</span> <span class="flag">single evidence</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="step-call"><pre><code>write.remember(db, {
  claim: 'The API uses GraphQL with a REST fallback.',
  source: 'inferred',
  category: 'fact',
});</code></pre></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="step-2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-2">
      <div class="step-text">
        <h3>Another session finds it</h3>
        <p>A day later a different session asks about the API and gets the belief back with the flags still on it. That is the point: the second agent sees a guess, not a fact.</p>
        <p>Recall is lexical: full-text search with rarer words weighted higher, plus character-trigram similarity. No model is called.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="belief flagged">
        <p class="belief-id">Belief #1 · fact · recalled</p>
        <p class="claim">The API uses GraphQL with a REST fallback.</p>
        <dl class="fields">
          <div><dt>source</dt><dd>inferred</dd></div>
          <div><dt>certainty</dt><dd>50%</dd></div>
          <div><dt>evidence</dt><dd>1</dd></div>
          <div><dt>freshness</dt><dd>99%</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="flags"><span class="flag">low trust</span> <span class="flag">single evidence</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="step-call"><pre><code>read.recall(db, 'graphql api');
read.listFlags(db);</code></pre></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="step-3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-3">
      <div class="step-text">
        <h3>Someone checks and corrects it</h3>
        <p>Someone looks at the API and REST is gone. In a markdown file you would now have two lines that disagree. Here the old belief is marked superseded and drops out of recall, the new belief starts at 80% because it was observed, and the lineage keeps both.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <div class="belief superseded">
          <p class="belief-id">Belief #1 · superseded by #2</p>
          <p class="claim">The API uses GraphQL with a REST fallback.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="belief confirmed">
          <p class="belief-id">Belief #2 · fact · supersedes #1</p>
          <p class="claim">The API uses GraphQL exclusively; REST was retired.</p>
          <dl class="fields">
            <div><dt>source</dt><dd>observed</dd></div>
            <div><dt>certainty</dt><dd>80%</dd></div>
            <div><dt>evidence</dt><dd>1</dd></div>
            <div><dt>freshness</dt><dd>100%</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="flags"><span class="flag">single evidence</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-call"><pre><code>write.correctBelief(db, 1, {
  claim: 'The API uses GraphQL exclusively; REST was retired.',
  source: 'observed',
  category: 'fact',
});</code></pre></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="step-4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-4">
      <div class="step-text">
        <h3>Ninety days pass</h3>
        <p>Nobody confirms the belief for 90 days. Freshness is down to 37% and the belief is flagged stale, so it turns up in review instead of steering every run.</p>
        <p>Confirm it and the clock starts over.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="belief flagged">
        <p class="belief-id">Belief #2 · fact · day 90</p>
        <p class="claim">The API uses GraphQL exclusively; REST was retired.</p>
        <dl class="fields">
          <div><dt>source</dt><dd>observed</dd></div>
          <div><dt>certainty</dt><dd>80%</dd></div>
          <div><dt>evidence</dt><dd>1</dd></div>
          <div><dt>freshness</dt><dd>37%</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="flags"><span class="flag">stale</span> <span class="flag">single evidence</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="step-call"><pre><code>read.listFlags(db);
// stale, single_evidence
write.confirmBelief(db, 2);</code></pre></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="step-5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-5">
      <div class="step-text">
        <h3>You retire it for good</h3>
        <p>Some things should not be remembered at all. Retire deletes the belief and its whole lineage: the call returns the deleted ids and recall comes back empty. Agents get the same call as the <code>retire_belief</code> tool.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="belief gone">
        <p class="belief-id">Beliefs #1 and #2 · deleted</p>
        <p class="claim">Nothing left to recall.</p>
        <p class="flags"><span class="flag ok">0 rows</span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="step-call"><pre><code>write.deleteBelief(db, 2);   // [1, 2]
read.recall(db, 'graphql api'); // []</code></pre></div>
    </div>
    <p class="drill-nav"><button type="button" class="button ghost" id="next-step" hidden>Next move</button></p>
  </section>

  <section class="record-form" aria-labelledby="record-title">
    <h2 id="record-title">What one belief records</h2>
    <p class="intro">Each line your markdown file would hold becomes one row. Open the file with <code>sqlite3</code> if you want to check.</p>
    <dl class="form">
      <div><dt>claim</dt><dd>The sentence itself, kept short.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>source</dt><dd>explicit, observed, distilled or inferred. Inferred and distilled beliefs are flagged low trust.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>category</dt><dd>What kind of belief it is, such as a fact or a preference.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>certainty</dt><dd>Starts from the source (explicit 90%, observed 80%, inferred 50%) and moves with confirmations.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>evidence</dt><dd>How many times it was confirmed. One piece of evidence is its own flag.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>freshness</dt><dd>Decays from the last confirmation; stale and fading flags are computed every time you read.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>lineage</dt><dd>Which belief it superseded, with a before and after diff.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="worry" aria-labelledby="worry-title">
    <h2 id="worry-title">If memory makes your agent worse</h2>
    <div class="worry-grid">
      <div><img class="shard" src="assets/images/art-480.webp" width="480" height="480" alt="A cracked shard of cyan light breaking up into violet particles." loading="lazy">
      <p>Plenty of developers turn agent memory off. Unread memories pile up, and an old line wins against a new one. That is a fair reason, and most of codex exists because of it.</p></div>
      <ul>
        <li>Recall returns only active beliefs. Superseded and forgotten ones stay out of the prompt.</li>
        <li>Flags tell you what to prune: stale, fading, low trust, single evidence.</li>
        <li>Every write lists nearby beliefs, so a near-duplicate shows up when it is written, not months later.</li>
        <li>Retire deletes a belief and its history. Forget keeps the history but removes it from recall.</li>
        <li>The database is one local file. No server, no API key, no network.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="numbers" aria-labelledby="numbers-title">
    <h2 id="numbers-title">What it costs to add</h2>
    <dl class="ledger wide">
      <div><dt>Runtime dependencies</dt><dd>0</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Node.js</dt><dd>24 or newer (node:sqlite)</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Size of dist/index.js, gzip</dt><dd>31.5 KB</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Import time</dt><dd>about 2.5 ms</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tests</dt><dd>380 passing</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Agent tools</dt><dd>7</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="note">Measured 2026-10-24 on the maintainer's machine with Node.js 24.14.0 against codex 0.3.2: size of the published dist/index.js at gzip -9, median of 11 cold imports, tests from the v0.3.2 tag.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="binaries" class="binaries" aria-labelledby="binaries-title">
    <h2 id="binaries-title">Install</h2>
    <p class="intro">The default way to run <code>ghostpaw-codex</code>: one executable per platform with Node.js inside, with the same commands and the same MCP server as the npm package. Each line downloads the latest release, checks it against the release&#39;s SHA256SUMS and installs the binary into <code>~/.local/bin</code>. The trailing <code>export</code> puts that folder on PATH for the current shell only; nothing edits <code>~/.profile</code>. The Windows script adds its folder to the user PATH.</p>
    <div class="bin-cmds">
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-linux-x64-label">linux-x64</p>
        <div class="cmd"><textarea id="bin-linux-x64" readonly rows="2" aria-labelledby="bin-linux-x64-label" spellcheck="false">curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GhostPawJS/codex/main/install.sh | sh -s -- --target linux-x64 &amp;&amp; export PATH=&quot;$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH&quot;</textarea><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-linux-x64">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-linux-arm64-label">linux-arm64</p>
        <div class="cmd"><textarea id="bin-linux-arm64" readonly rows="2" aria-labelledby="bin-linux-arm64-label" spellcheck="false">curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GhostPawJS/codex/main/install.sh | sh -s -- --target linux-arm64 &amp;&amp; export PATH=&quot;$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH&quot;</textarea><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-linux-arm64">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-darwin-arm64-label">darwin-arm64</p>
        <div class="cmd"><textarea id="bin-darwin-arm64" readonly rows="2" aria-labelledby="bin-darwin-arm64-label" spellcheck="false">curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GhostPawJS/codex/main/install.sh | sh -s -- --target darwin-arm64 &amp;&amp; export PATH=&quot;$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH&quot;</textarea><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-darwin-arm64">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-darwin-x64-label">darwin-x64</p>
        <div class="cmd"><textarea id="bin-darwin-x64" readonly rows="2" aria-labelledby="bin-darwin-x64-label" spellcheck="false">curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GhostPawJS/codex/main/install.sh | sh -s -- --target darwin-x64 &amp;&amp; export PATH=&quot;$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH&quot;</textarea><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-darwin-x64">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-windows-x64-label">windows-x64 (PowerShell)</p>
        <div class="cmd"><textarea id="bin-windows-x64" readonly rows="2" aria-labelledby="bin-windows-x64-label" spellcheck="false">irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GhostPawJS/codex/main/install.ps1 | iex</textarea><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-windows-x64">Copy</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="bin-cmds">
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-profile-label">Keep it on PATH in new shells (appends one line to ~/.profile)</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-profile" readonly value="echo 'export PATH=&quot;$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH&quot;' &gt;&gt; ~/.profile" aria-labelledby="bin-profile-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-profile">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-pin-label">Pin a release instead of the latest (add --version)</p>
        <div class="cmd"><textarea id="bin-pin" readonly rows="2" aria-labelledby="bin-pin-label" spellcheck="false">curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GhostPawJS/codex/main/install.sh | sh -s -- --target linux-x64 --version 0.6.0 &amp;&amp; export PATH=&quot;$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH&quot;</textarea><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-pin">Copy</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <dl class="ledger wide">
      <div><dt>linux-x64</dt><dd>41.7 MB tar.gz</dd></div>
      <div><dt>linux-arm64</dt><dd>41.2 MB tar.gz</dd></div>
      <div><dt>darwin-arm64</dt><dd>44.8 MB tar.gz</dd></div>
      <div><dt>darwin-x64</dt><dd>46.4 MB tar.gz</dd></div>
      <div><dt>windows-x64</dt><dd>38.4 MB zip</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="note">Archive sizes of the v0.5.2 release assets. Linux builds need glibc 2.28 or newer; on Alpine, use npx.</p>
    <h3>Other release installers</h3>
    <div class="bin-cmds">
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-mise-label">mise</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-mise" readonly value="mise use -g github:GhostPawJS/codex" aria-labelledby="bin-mise-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-mise">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-ubi-label">ubi</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-ubi" readonly value="ubi --project GhostPawJS/codex --exe ghostpaw-codex --in ~/.local/bin" aria-labelledby="bin-ubi-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-ubi">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-eget-label">eget</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-eget" readonly value="eget GhostPawJS/codex" aria-labelledby="bin-eget-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-eget">Copy</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <h3>npx / npm (for npm-based environments)</h3>
    <div class="bin-cmds">
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-npx-label">npx, with Node.js 24 or newer</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-npx" readonly value="npx -y @ghostpaw/codex --help" aria-labelledby="bin-npx-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-npx">Copy</button></div></div>
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-npm-label">Global npm install</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-npm" readonly value="npm install -g @ghostpaw/codex" aria-labelledby="bin-npm-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-npm">Copy</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <h3>Library (for harnesses embedding it in code)</h3>
    <div class="bin-cmds">
        <div><p class="cmd-label" id="bin-lib-label">Add it to a Node.js 24+ project</p>
        <div class="cmd"><input id="bin-lib" readonly value="npm install @ghostpaw/codex" aria-labelledby="bin-lib-label" spellcheck="false"><button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="bin-lib">Copy</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <ul class="bin-trust">
      <li>install.sh and install.ps1 check each archive against SHA256SUMS and stop on a mismatch.</li>
      <li>Each archive and SHA256SUMS carry a GitHub build attestation. Check one with <code>gh attestation verify &lt;archive&gt; -R GhostPawJS/codex</code>.</li>
      <li>The binaries are not code signed. macOS builds carry an ad-hoc signature, so a browser download needs <code>xattr -d com.apple.quarantine ghostpaw-codex</code>. Windows may show a SmartScreen warning for a browser download.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="shot">
      <a href="{{.urls.cliShot}}"><img src="assets/images/cli-1600.webp" srcset="assets/images/cli-800.webp 800w, assets/images/cli-1600.webp 1600w" sizes="(min-width: 68rem) 64rem, 100vw" width="1600" height="790" decoding="async" alt="Terminal session with ghostpaw-codex 0.5.2: --version --json prints name, version, variant and target; belief add prints a compact block; search for what class does fox play shares only common words, so it returns three beliefs and a low-signal warning; search for which warrior does fox main returns Fox mains a Warrior; after alias add class warrior, the first query returns that belief too." loading="lazy"></a>
      <figcaption>A session with the 0.5.2 linux-x64 binary against a small database of five beliefs. Open the image for full size.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="links"><a class="more" href="{{.urls.release}}">Release v0.5.2</a><a href="{{.urls.readmeInstall}}">Install notes in the README</a><a href="{{.urls.attest}}">gh attestation verify</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="start" aria-labelledby="start-title">
    <h2 id="start-title">Start from the README</h2>
    <pre><code>import { DatabaseSync } from 'node:sqlite';
import { initCodexTables, read, write } from '@ghostpaw/codex';

const db = new DatabaseSync('codex.db');
initCodexTables(db);

const { id } = write.remember(db, {
  claim: 'The API uses GraphQL with a REST fallback.',
  source: 'explicit',
  category: 'fact',
});
write.confirmBelief(db, id);
read.recall(db, 'graphql api');
read.listFlags(db);</code></pre>
    <p>The seven tools (<code>search_codex</code>, <code>review_codex</code>, <code>inspect_codex_item</code>, <code>remember_belief</code>, <code>revise_belief</code>, <code>retire_belief</code>, <code>dismiss_proximity</code>) ship with JSON Schemas for any LLM SDK. The MCP server and the command line tool use the same seven.</p>
    <p class="links"><a class="more" href="{{.urls.readme}}">Read the README</a><a href="{{.urls.demo}}">Try the live Belief Console</a><a href="{{.urls.npm}}">codex on npm</a></p>
    <nav class="docs-map" aria-labelledby="docs-map-title">
      <h3 id="docs-map-title">{{.data.docs.heading}}</h3>
      <ul>
        {{range .data.docs.pages}}
        <li><a href="{{index $.urls .key}}">{{.label}}</a><span>{{.note}}</span></li>
        {{end}}
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </section>
